msgid ":doc:`/usage/dependency-injection`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:546
msgid "Dependency overrides in tests"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:548
msgid ""
"FastAPI exposes ``app.dependency_overrides`` for swapping dependencies on a "
"live application. Litestar does not. The omission is deliberate: an override "
"that mutates a hared application is hard to reason about under parallel "
"tests, and Litestar is of the opinion that it's better to structure the "
"application in such a way that overrides are not necessary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:554
msgid "Alternative strategies are, order of preference:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:556
msgid ""
"Build the application from a factory that takes the dependencies your tests "
"need to swap. Natural choice when the override exists to replace a real "
"database with a test one"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:559
msgid ""
"Construct a fresh application per test with :func:`~litestar.testing."
"create_test_client`, passing the overridden dependency through the same "
"``dependencies=`` keyword the production application uses"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:562
msgid ""
"Patch the dependency callable with a mocking library: A last resort reserved "
"for cases where first two are impractical reach"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:567
msgid "Exceptions and error responses"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:569
msgid ""
"Both frameworks export an ``HTTPException``. The FastAPI one takes the "
"status code as a positional argument; the Litestar one uses the keyword "
"argument ``status_code``. A positional argument to Litestar's :class:"
"`~litestar.exceptions.HTTPException` is appended to ``detail``, which is "
"rendered in the JSON response."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:580
msgid ""
"from fastapi import HTTPException\n"
"\n"
"@app.get(\"/\")\n"
"async def index() -> None:\n"
"    raise HTTPException(400, detail=\"can't find that\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:591
msgid ""
"from litestar import Litestar, get\n"
"from litestar.exceptions import HTTPException\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@get(\"/\")\n"
"async def index() -> None:\n"
"    response_fields = {\"array\": \"value\"}\n"
"    raise HTTPException(\n"
"        status_code=400,\n"
"        detail=f\"can't get that field: {response_fields.get('missing')}\",\n"
"    )\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"app = Litestar(route_handlers=[index])"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:595
msgid "Custom exception handlers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:597
msgid ""
"FastAPI registers exception handlers through ``@app.exception_handler``. "
"Litestar accepts a mapping from exception class or status code to a handler "
"callable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:601
msgid ""
"Exception handling is part of Litestar's layered architecture, so these can "
"be declared on applications, routers, controller or route handlers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:610
msgid ""
"class ItemNotFound(Exception): ...\n"
"\n"
"@app.exception_handler(ItemNotFound)\n"
"async def handle(request, exc) -> JSONResponse:\n"
"    return JSONResponse({\"detail\": \"item not found\"}, status_code=404)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:621
msgid ""
"from litestar import Litestar, Request, Response, get\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"class ItemNotFoundError(Exception):\n"
"    pass\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"def handle_item_not_found(request: Request, exception: ItemNotFoundError) -> "
"Response[dict[str, str]]:\n"
"    return Response({\"detail\": \"item not found\"}, status_code=404)\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@get(\"/\")\n"
"async def index() -> None:\n"
"    raise ItemNotFoundError\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"app = Litestar(\n"
"    route_handlers=[index],\n"
"    exception_handlers={ItemNotFoundError: handle_item_not_found},\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:625
msgid ":ref:`usage/exceptions:exception handling`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:629 ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:894
msgid "Authentication"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:631
msgid ""
"FastAPI usually handles authentication through dependency injection. The "
"same approach work in Litestar, but the more idiomatic choice is a guard or "
"a custom :doc:`/usage/security/abstract-authentication-middleware`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:635
msgid "Info"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:637
msgid ""
"A guard is a callable that receives the :class:`~litestar.connection."
"ASGIConnection` and the :class:`~litestar.handlers.BaseRouteHandler`; "
"raising an exception here aborts the request."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:642
msgid ""
"Guards are part of Litestar's layered architecture, so these can be declared "
"on applications, routers, controller or route handlers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:651
msgid ""
"from fastapi import Depends\n"
"\n"
"async def authenticate(request: Request) -> None: ...\n"
"\n"
"@app.get(\"/\", dependencies=[Depends(authenticate)])\n"
"async def index() -> dict[str, str]: ..."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:663
msgid ""
"from litestar import Litestar, get\n"
"from litestar.connection import ASGIConnection\n"
"from litestar.exceptions import NotAuthorizedException\n"
"from litestar.handlers import BaseRouteHandler\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"async def require_token(connection: ASGIConnection, route_handler: "
"BaseRouteHandler) -> None:\n"
"    if connection.headers.get(\"authorization\") != \"Bearer secret\":\n"
"        raise NotAuthorizedException\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@get(\"/\", guards=[require_token])\n"
"async def index() -> dict[str, str]:\n"
"    return {\"hello\": \"world\"}\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"app = Litestar(route_handlers=[index])"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:667
msgid ":doc:`/usage/security/index`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:671 ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:898
msgid "Middleware"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:673
msgid ""
"Pure ASGI middleware - a callable that wraps an ASGI app and returns another "
"- works the same in both frameworks. Middleware built on Starlette's "
"``BaseHTTPMiddleware`` does not port directly: use :class:`~litestar."
"middleware.ASGIMiddleware` instead. Subclass it and implement :meth:"
"`~litestar.middleware.ASGIMiddleware.handle`, which receives ``scope``, "
"``receive``, ``send``, and a ``next_app`` callable (the equivalent of "
"``call_next``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:686
msgid ""
"from starlette.middleware.base import BaseHTTPMiddleware\n"
"\n"
"class ProcessTimeMiddleware(BaseHTTPMiddleware):\n"
"    async def dispatch(self, request, call_next):\n"
"        start = time.monotonic()\n"
"        response = await call_next(request)\n"
"        response.headers[\"x-process-time\"] = f\"{time.monotonic() - "
"start:.4f}\"\n"
"        return response\n"
"\n"
"app.add_middleware(ProcessTimeMiddleware)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:702
msgid ""
"import time\n"
"\n"
"from litestar import Litestar, get\n"
"from litestar.datastructures import MutableScopeHeaders\n"
"from litestar.middleware import ASGIMiddleware\n"
"from litestar.types import ASGIApp, Message, Receive, Scope, Send\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"class ProcessTimeMiddleware(ASGIMiddleware):\n"
"    async def handle(self, scope: Scope, receive: Receive, send: Send, "
"next_app: ASGIApp) -> None:\n"
"        start_time = time.monotonic()\n"
"\n"
"        async def send_wrapper(message: Message) -> None:\n"
"            if message[\"type\"] == \"http.response.start\":\n"
"                headers = MutableScopeHeaders.from_message(message=message)\n"
"                headers[\"x-process-time\"] = f\"{time.monotonic() - "
"start_time:.4f}\"\n"
"            await send(message)\n"
"\n"
"        await next_app(scope, receive, send_wrapper)\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@get(\"/\")\n"
"async def index() -> dict[str, str]:\n"
"    return {\"hello\": \"world\"}\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"app = Litestar(route_handlers=[index], middleware=[ProcessTimeMiddleware()])"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:706
msgid ":doc:`/usage/middleware/creating-middleware`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:710
msgid "Background tasks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:712
msgid ""
"FastAPI injects a ``BackgroundTasks`` object into the handler. In Litestar, "
"a :class:`~litestar.background_tasks.BackgroundTask` (or a :class:`~litestar."
"background_tasks.BackgroundTasks` collection) can be passed directly to the "
"response. These tasks run after the response body has been sent."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:723
msgid ""
"from fastapi import BackgroundTasks\n"
"\n"
"@app.get(\"/\")\n"
"async def greeter(tasks: BackgroundTasks) -> dict[str, str]:\n"
"    tasks.add_task(log_visit, \"world\")\n"
"    return {\"hello\": \"world\"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:735
msgid ""
"import logging\n"
"\n"
"from litestar import Litestar, Response, get\n"
"from litestar.background_tasks import BackgroundTask\n"
"\n"
"logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"async def log_visit(name: str) -> None:\n"
"    logger.info(\"greeted %s\", name)\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@get(\"/\")\n"
"async def greeter() -> Response[dict[str, str]]:\n"
"    return Response(\n"
"        {\"hello\": \"world\"},\n"
"        background=BackgroundTask(log_visit, \"world\"),\n"
"    )\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"app = Litestar(route_handlers=[greeter])"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:739
msgid "WebSockets"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:741
msgid ""
"FastAPI exposes a single ``@app.websocket`` decorator for direct connection "
"handling. Litestar offers three handler styles for the three patterns that "
"recur in WebSocket code:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:744
msgid ""
":func:`~litestar.handlers.websocket`: Raw `ASGI WebSocket <https://asgi."
"readthedocs.io/en/latest/specs/www.html#websocket>`_ handling, same "
"semantics as ``@app.websocket``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:747
msgid ""
":func:`~litestar.handlers.websocket_listener`: Per-message callback style "
"that takes and returns typed values; the framework handles accept, the "
"receive loop, and serialisation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:750
msgid ""
":func:`~litestar.handlers.websocket_stream`: Async generator to produce "
"messages that are pushed to the WebSocket;  the framework handles accept, "
"the receive loop, and serialisation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:760
msgid ""
"from fastapi import WebSocket\n"
"\n"
"@app.websocket(\"/ws\")\n"
"async def echo(socket: WebSocket) -> None:\n"
"    await socket.accept()\n"
"    while True:\n"
"        data = await socket.receive_json()\n"
"        await socket.send_json({\"echo\": data[\"message\"]})"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:774
msgid ""
"from litestar import Litestar, websocket_listener\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@websocket_listener(\"/ws\")\n"
"async def echo(data: dict[str, str]) -> dict[str, str]:\n"
"    return {\"echo\": data[\"message\"]}\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"app = Litestar(route_handlers=[echo])"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:776
msgid ""
"For broadcast and pub/sub patterns, pair these handlers with the :doc:"
"`channels </usage/channels>` plugin. Channels handles per-channel "
"subscriptions, history, and inter-process fan-out through a pluggable "
"broker, and can generate WebSocket route handlers that publish incoming "
"events to subscribed clients."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:783
msgid ":doc:`/usage/websockets`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:787
msgid "Testing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:789
msgid ""
"Both frameworks ship an ``httpx``-based ``TestClient``. The Litestar one is :"
"class:`~litestar.testing.TestClient`. For unit testing, Litestar also "
"provides :func:`~litestar.testing.create_test_client` and :func:`~litestar."
"testing.create_async_test_client`, which take the same arguments as :class:"
"`Litestar` and return a configured client."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:801
msgid ""
"from fastapi.testclient import TestClient\n"
"\n"
"def test_index() -> None:\n"
"    with TestClient(app) as client:\n"
"        response = client.get(\"/\")\n"
"    assert response.status_code == 200"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/fastapi.rst:813
msgid ""
"from litestar import get\n"
"from litestar.status_codes import HTTP_200_OK\n"
"from litestar.testing import create_test_client\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@get(\"/\", sync_to_thread=False)\n"
"def hello() -> dict[str, str]:\n"
"    return {\"hello\": \"world\"}\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"def test_hello() -> None:\n"
"    with create_test_client(route_handlers=[hello]) as client:\n"
"        response = client.get(\"/\")\n"
"    assert response.status_code == HTTP_200_OK\n"
"    assert response.json() == {\"hello\": \"world\"}"
msgstr ""

